# Layer 2: Aurora Command Labs — The R&D / Doctrine Layer

This is where the ACT principles live without scaring enterprise customers too early.

**Domain:** `auroracommandlabs.com`

Purpose:

```text
Research, prototypes, white papers, field books, onboarding material,
and command system doctrine.
```

This is where you publish:

```text
Lawful Command Systems
GPU Infrastructure Field Book
AI Pipeline Failure Taxonomy
Command Deck Core Architecture
Symbol Analysis Field Book
Carbon/Silicon Interface Notes
```

### Why a lawful command system matters

The future is not short of AI.

The future is short of **lawful action**.

AI systems can generate answers, summarize logs, recommend commands, write code, and steer automation. But when AI touches infrastructure, robotics, customer data, finance, facilities, energy, medical systems, or space systems, the problem becomes:

```text
Who authorized this?
What did it affect?
What evidence proves the result?
Can it be reversed?
Who carries consequence?
Was the system allowed to act?
```

That is the core business argument.

```text
The next generation of AI infrastructure will need command law,
not just model intelligence.
```

### ACT-derived operating principles

| ACT Principle | Business Translation                                |
| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Field         | Know the environment before acting                  |
| Law           | Define what may and may not happen                  |
| Gateway       | Require authority before consequence-bearing action |
| Fabric        | Route commands through known paths                  |
| Evidence      | Record what happened and why                        |
| Memory        | Convert incidents into system learning              |
| Coherence     | Keep identity and purpose stable under change       |

This becomes the Pykesys doctrine:

```text
No command without authority.
No authority without scope.
No action without evidence.
No evidence without memory.
No memory without learning.
```

---

# Layer 3: Aurora Core Bridge — The Product / Interface Layer

**Domain:** `auroracorebridge.com`

This is the product layer.

At first, it should not claim to be a full command system. It should begin as a **console**.

Your first core:

```text
NVL72 Console / Touch Screen Command Core
```

Even if you do not physically own NVL72 hardware, you can build the conceptual and operational console for it.

NVIDIA’s GB200 NVL72 architecture is a rack-scale design with 72 Blackwell GPUs connected into a large NVLink domain; this kind of hardware turns AI infrastructure into a giant coordinated machine rather than a loose pile of servers. ([Wikipedia][1]) NVLink itself is NVIDIA’s high-speed GPU/CPU interconnect, distinct from ordinary PCI Express, and higher GPU counts use NVSwitch-style fabrics. ([Wikipedia][2])

That means the console opportunity is real:

```text
The more unified the machine becomes,
the more important command visibility becomes.
```

## First Core: NVL72 Console

### What it is

```text
A touch-oriented operational console for rack-scale GPU systems,
showing hardware, fabric, jobs, checkpoints, health, and command authority.
```

### It does not need to control real NVL72 at first

Start with:

```text
simulated NVL72 topology
mock GPU health data
synthetic PyTorch job state
checkpoint/resume events
fabric degradation scenarios
dashboard result pipeline
```

Then later add adapters:

```text
NVIDIA DCGM
Kubernetes
Run:ai
Prometheus
Slurm
Redfish
InfiniBand counters
storage telemetry
```

Run:ai matters here because it is Kubernetes-based GPU orchestration for allocating, scheduling, sharing, and managing AI workloads. ([Wikipedia][3]) In the Pykesys model, Run:ai is not the whole command system; it is one scheduler/control-plane input to the lawful console.

### The first visual model

```text
Aurora Core Bridge
  NVL72 Console
    Rack View
    GPU Fabric View
    Job Pipeline View
    Checkpoint View
    Customer Result View
    Evidence View
    Command Gateway View
```

### Why customers might care

Small AI shops and GPU-heavy startups often have the same failure:

```text
They know the model output.
They do not know the operational truth.
```

Aurora Core Bridge would answer:

```text
What is running?
Where is it running?
What stage is it in?
What GPU/fabric/storage path does it depend on?
What checkpoint exists?
What failed?
What can be resumed?
What evidence proves the customer result?
```

That is valuable.

